  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nLatest radar indicated light to moderate rain across most of S TX \nwith a few heavier pockets across the Coastal Bend this afternoon. \nFor tonight, lower rainfall amounts/weak showers are forecast across \nmost of S TX with higher amounts mostly confined across the southern \nCoastal Bend close to the coast. Due to the more isolated nature of \nheavy rainfall expected, the Flash Flood Watch was allowed to \nexpire. The Weather Prediction Center has a slight risk of excessive \nrainfall along the southern Coastal Bend through tonight. Models \ndiffer on rain chances for tonight through Tuesday with the latest \nCAM models indicating isolated light showers and low precip amounts \nacross all of S TX, while the GFS, ECMWF and Canadian all show more \nscattered and heavier rainfall across the southern Coastal Bend. \nGiven the mid level low is forecast to shift northeast overnight, \nbut still provide diffluence aloft, the surface trough will continue \nto provide low level convergence along the coast and PWATs 1.7-2.0 \ninches forecast to remain across the southern Coastal Bend, \nconfidence is moderate to high that pockets of higher rainfall \namounts of 0.25-0.75\" will remain possible tonight generally closer \nto the coast from Corpus Christi to Kingsville to Baffin Bay and \neastward. The heaviest rainfall will likely be south of the CWA and \nover the coastal waters where there is better instability. \n\nSo, overall will keep the medium to high rain chances across S TX \nwith mainly light showers and low qpf, except slightly higher near \nthe coast of the southern Coastal Bend. This activity is expected to \ncontinue through Tuesday morning, then decreasing to a low chance \nmost areas by Tuesday afternoon. Low to medium rain chances continue \ninto Wednesday, becoming low to very low chance the latter part of \nthe week.\n\nMax Highs will remain below normal through Tuesday with warming to \nnear normal values by mid week. Lows are expected to generally be at \nor above normal through Wednesday. Models are indicating a cold \nfront Thursday or Friday bringing mainly drier conditions to S TX. \nHighs will continue at normal levels while the min temperatures drop \ninto the 50s for Thursday night and Friday night.\n\nAnother concern continues to be minor coastal flooding along gulf \nfacing beaches during high tide this afternoon into tonight. Latest \nP-ETSS run indicated that tide levels may exceed the 2ft MSL \nthreshold during high tide tonight with with up to 2.2 feet above \nMSL possible. Tide levels are expected to be lower through the \nremainder of the week due to lower wind speeds across the gulf \nwaters. A moderate rip current risk expected tonight, becoming low \nTuesday.",
